Local Debian 13 package
======================

This package follows the local btop packaging workflow: debhelper 13, Debian
build flags, source packages and an unprivileged sbuild Trixie chroot. VERSION
defines the upstream version; debian/changelog supplies the Debian revision.
The package includes the GUI, native tools and private Linux 6.12 ALSA sources.
It builds only the external module through DKMS, never the installed kernel.

Install build tools:
  sudo apt install sbuild mmdebstrap uidmap devscripts debhelper lintian

Create the build environment as a regular user, or reuse the btop chroot:
  mkdir -p ~/.cache/sbuild
  mmdebstrap --mode=unshare --variant=buildd --include=ca-certificates \
    --aptopt='APT::Update::Error-Mode "any"' --arch=amd64 --format=tar \
    --skip=output/mknod trixie ~/.cache/sbuild/trixie-amd64.tar \
    'deb https://deb.debian.org/debian trixie main' \
    'deb https://deb.debian.org/debian trixie-updates main' \
    'deb https://security.debian.org/debian-security trixie-security main'

Build from the checkout, including intended uncommitted source changes:
  python3 tools/build-debian.py --jobs 2
The output directory must be new. Git-ignored files, manufacturer downloads,
local analysis and previous build artifacts are excluded from the source.
The source package records its exact inputs and can be rebuilt independently.

Inspect and simulate installation before installing the amd64 package:
  lintian build/debian-trixie/*.changes
  sudo apt install --simulate ./build/debian-trixie/octa-capture_*_amd64.deb
  sudo apt install ./build/debian-trixie/octa-capture_*_amd64.deb

Installation configures DKMS, device-specific udev access, automatic dedicated
driver activation and the Xfce launcher. The packaged stereo-pair service runs
as each desktop user. Its first start retires only the exact legacy unit
created by octa-pulse-pairs --install, retaining a backup; custom units remain.
Personal device settings and presets are not removed with the package.
The supported kernel series is 6.12; Secure Boot requires the DKMS signing key
to be enrolled according to the administrator's Debian setup.
